---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import GatewayCard from '@/components/ia/GatewayCard.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Trust, governance and limitations"
  description="Understand claims and evidence, PII annotations, governance boundaries, limitations and the known-issues register for the PDTF 1.0-derived ontology."
>
  <PageMeta category="PDTF 1.0 · extracted ontology" variant="warn" updated="Draft · under review" />
  <h1>Trust, governance and limitations</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Trust requires more than a provenance label. This category explains how the draft models
    claims, evidence, verification and assurance; how it annotates personal-data concerns; and
    where its limitations, consumer traps, by-design gaps and unresolved validator caveats are
    recorded rather than hidden.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">PDTF 1.0-derived draft with explicit limitations</p>
    <p>
      This semantic corpus is not an approved SPDTF 2.0 ontology, a runtime authorisation
      system, a legal compliance decision or proof that source data is true. Its governance
      annotations and deterministic checks have only the authority and scope stated on their
      pages. Read the known-issues register before relying on the model.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="separate">Keep four concerns separate</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">What the corpus records</th><th scope="col">What remains outside it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Claim provenance</th><td>PROV-O derivation, verification activities, evidence roles and assurance-related concepts.</td><td>Whether a claim is factually true in a particular transaction.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Sensitivity</th><td>DPV references and PII-bearing annotations kept outside the class and shapes graphs.</td><td>Runtime access control, consent operations and organisation-specific legal assessment.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Governance</th><td>Recorded modelling decisions, separation rules and page-level status.</td><td>Approval of SPDTF 2.0 meaning or promotion to a governed release.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Limitations</th><td>Dated caveats, thin profiles, inference traps, validator settings and re-open triggers.</td><td>A guarantee that no unrecorded defect or consumer-specific risk exists.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="review">Review trust and limitations</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/claims" title="Claims and evidence model" eyebrow="Provenance and assurance" description="Inspect the PROV-O backbone, evidence roles, verification activities and the separate assurance layer." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/governance" title="Governance and PII model" eyebrow="Sensitivity annotations" description="Review DPV references, PII-bearing kinds and the three-graph boundary that keeps advice separate from semantics and constraints." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/known-issues" title="Limitations and known issues" eyebrow="Required reading" description="Read the dated register of consumer traps, by-design gaps, report caveats, validator requirements and re-open triggers." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="operating-governance">Semantic governance is not programme governance</h2>
  <p>
    The ontology pages describe governance concepts and the decisions that shaped this corpus.
    Cross-programme decision rights, lifecycle and promotion rules belong in the site’s
    <a href="/governance">Governance</a> destination. For the audit trail connecting individual
    modelling choices to records, use
    <a href="/ontology/lineage-and-verification">Lineage, provenance and verification</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
